// GitHub issue #452: a value that cannot be reached without its lock.
//
// THE PROBLEM THIS EXISTS FOR
//
// Issue #479's Group A added five locks in one session, and every one of
// them expressed what it protects in a comment:
//
//     private let mut asid_next: usize = 1;
//     private let mut asid_lock: Mutex;      // protects asid_next
//
// The compiler does not know those two lines are related. A new call site
// that reads asid_next without acquiring asid_lock compiles, and nothing
// in this repository says anything. On one core that comment was true and
// unfalsifiable in the same breath, because nothing else could be
// running; as of issue #447 a second core takes interrupts, and issue
// #479's Group B runs a process there, at which point "the caller holds
// the lock" stops being a property of the machine and becomes a property
// of each call site -- which is what a comment is worst at carrying.
//
// WHAT THIS CHANGES
//
// The value moves INSIDE a struct whose field is private to this file, so
// no other file can name it at all. The only way to obtain a pointer to
// it is locked_cell_value, which needs a `borrow LockedCellGuard[id]`,
// and the only way to obtain one of those is locked_cell_take on THAT
// cell. Three separate compile errors follow, none of which needed a
// convention:
//
//   - reaching the value with no guard is a missing argument;
//   - reaching it with ANOTHER cell's guard is a static value mismatch,
//     because `[cell: addr]` carries the identity of the cell that minted
//     it (issue #351's mechanism, unchanged);
//   - using the returned pointer after locked_cell_give is "pointer 'v'
//     is derived from linear value 'g' and cannot be used after 'g' is
//     consumed", which is the half a `private` marker alone never gave.
//
// And forgetting to release is a compile error on every path including
// early returns, because the guard is linear.
//
// WHY THE VALUE IS THE FIRST FIELD
//
// Deliberate, and it is the one layout decision here. Issue #479 lost an
// RPi5 boot to this twice: an 8-byte lock word at the FRONT of an
// `align(16)` aggregate shifts every array behind it to 8-mod-16, and
// LLVM may then emit a 128-bit store that the MMU-off window cannot use.
// The convention that came out of that was "the lock goes in its own
// global, never as a field". With `value` at offset 0 the cell inherits
// T's own alignment and T's interior is laid out exactly as it would be
// standalone, so wrapping an aligned aggregate is safe and that
// convention does not have to be re-litigated per call site.
// `--emit-struct-layout` is how to check it for a given T.
//
// WHY NOT pool_lock_acquire
//
// The same reason kernel/lib/intrusive_pool.tkb's tag counter gives: a
// tag registers the acquire in linux_user's lock-ORDER table, that table
// is finite, and a cell is not a pool. Putting non-pools in it pushed
// real pool acquires out as "untracked" once already.
//
// Current limitations (2026-08-30). Keep this list current as call sites
// change; a limitation that goes stale here is worse than none, since it
// invites relying on a guarantee that quietly stopped holding.
// - IT PROVES THE DISCIPLINE, NOT THE ASSIGNMENT. That the value in this
//   cell is the data this lock is supposed to protect is still a module
//   obligation -- the same caveat kernel/net/tcp.tkb's retx_retry_state
//   carries, and SPEC.md states it under "Guard-derived pointers". What
//   is machine-checked is that nobody reaches the value without holding
//   the guard, and that no path forgets to give it back.
// - ONE VALUE, ONE LOCK. Two cells are two locks. A critical section
//   spanning two cells takes two guards and therefore has an ORDER,
//   which nothing here checks (issue #466). Prefer one cell holding a
//   struct of everything the lock covers -- that is the shape
//   kernel/arch/arm64/mm/asid.tkb uses.
// - NO READER/WRITER DISTINCTION. locked_cell_value hands out a mutable
//   `*T`; a read-only holder gets the same exclusion a writer does.
// - locked_cell_value_boot IS AN ESCAPE HATCH and is `!{unsafe}` so that
//   every use is visible in the trusted-base inventory. It exists for the
//   MMU-off window, where a lock cannot be taken at all (spin_lock is
//   `!{requires_mmu}`: AArch64 has no exclusives on Device-typed memory)
//   and does not need to be, since that code runs before PSCI has been
//   asked for a second core. Nothing checks that a caller is really in
//   that window.
// - THE GUARD IS 8 STACK BYTES while held, carrying the interrupt state
//   the release must restore -- issue #451's `flags`, which is where
//   Linux's `unsigned long flags` lives too.
use "kernel/lib/mutex.tkb";

generic struct LockedCell(T: type) {
    // First, on purpose. See "WHY THE VALUE IS THE FIRST FIELD" above.
    private value: T;
    private lock: Mutex;
}

// Private: only this file may mint one, which is what makes "you hold a
// guard" mean "you called locked_cell_take". `[cell: addr]` is the
// identity of the cell it came from.
private linear struct LockedCellGuard[cell: addr] {
    private flags: usize;
}

// Acquires this cell. No mutex_init is needed or wanted for a cell in a
// global: .bss is zeroed and a zeroed word is SPIN_LOCK_FREE, and
// mutex_init FORCE-FREES, which on two cores releases a lock somebody
// else is holding. kernel/lib/mutex.tkb has the whole argument, and
// scripts/check_lock_discipline.py makes forgetting it a build failure.
fn locked_cell_take(T: type, cell: &mut LockedCell(T) @ id)
        -> LockedCellGuard[id] {
    let mut g: LockedCellGuard[id] = { mutex_acquire(&(*cell).lock) };
    return g;
}

// Linear, so forgetting this is a compile error on every path.
fn locked_cell_give(T: type, cell: &mut LockedCell(T) @ id,
                    g: sink LockedCellGuard[id]) {
    mutex_release(&(*cell).lock, g.flags);
}

// The only way to reach the value. The returned pointer dies with the
// guard.
fn locked_cell_value(T: type, g: borrow LockedCellGuard[id],
                     cell: &mut LockedCell(T) @ id) -> *T @ id {
    return &(*cell).value;
}

// Observability, and public for the same reason spin_lock_is_held is: a
// lock that never actually took would pass a take/give smoke test and
// fail this. linux_user/locked_cell is the caller.
fn locked_cell_is_held(T: type, cell: &mut LockedCell(T) @ id)
        -> bool !{unsafe} {
    return mutex_is_held(&(*cell).lock);
}

// -- One lock, many values --------------------------------------------
//
// LockedCell above is one value beside one lock, which fits a counter.
// The other shape issue #452 has to serve is one lock covering a FIELD OF
// EVERY OBJECT in a pool -- kernel/kernel/fd_table.tkb's SharedObject.refs
// is the case, where a per-object lock word would cost 8 bytes times every
// open file to shorten a critical section that is already a compare and an
// add.
//
// Marking the field `private` in its own struct does not do it, because
// `private` is per FILE: the accessor and every other function in that
// 2400-line file are the same file, so the field stays reachable from all
// of them. Checked by trying it -- an unguarded read added to
// fd_table.tkb compiled. The field's TYPE has to belong to a different
// file, which is what this one is for.
//
// WHAT THIS DOES NOT CARRY, and it is the difference from LockedCell.
// LockedCell's guard is minted from the cell, so `[cell: addr]` proves the
// guard is THAT cell's. Here the lock is separate from the data by
// construction, so a guard proves only that SOME lock was taken. With one
// adopter and one lock that is vacuous; with two, nothing stops one
// subsystem's guard opening another's field. If a second adopter appears,
// that is the moment to give it a lock identity of its own
// rather than the moment to notice.
//
// Generic because the access discipline is independent of the protected
// value's representation. This remains usable inside a pooled payload:
// monomorphization resolves the field before sizeof(T) computes the
// enclosing payload's storage size.
generic struct GuardedField(T: type) {
    private value: T;
}

private linear struct GuardedFieldGuard[lock: addr] {
    private flags: usize;
}

// mutex_acquire, not pool_lock_acquire, for the reason at the top of this
// file.
fn guarded_field_take(m: *Mutex @ id) -> GuardedFieldGuard[id] {
    let mut g: GuardedFieldGuard[id] = { mutex_acquire(m) };
    return g;
}

fn guarded_field_give(g: sink GuardedFieldGuard[id], m: *Mutex @ id) {
    mutex_release(m, g.flags);
}

// The only way to the value. Tied to the guard, so it dies with it.
fn guarded_usize_value(g: borrow GuardedFieldGuard[lock],
                       f: *GuardedField(usize)) -> *usize @ lock {
    return &(*f).value;
}

// The MMU-off window's way in, and the only one. See the limitation above
// for why it is `unsafe` rather than merely documented.
//
// A plain `*T`, with no `@ id`: there is no guard here for a pointer to
// be tied to, and saying so is the point. The compiler agrees -- a return
// annotation naming a region needs a borrow parameter that supplies it,
// so writing `@ id` on this one does not type-check. What a caller gets
// is an ordinary pointer with an ordinary lifetime, which is exactly the
// weaker thing an escape hatch should hand back.
fn locked_cell_value_boot(T: type, cell: &mut LockedCell(T) @ id)
        -> *T !{unsafe} {
    return &(*cell).value;
}
